{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3050","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3050","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3050\",\"parent_revision\":\"e370e30a6d345b3347cd2ee355a7f195c719174e4e32dc4819a4d36ca4d9b0f9\",\"root_revision\":\"e370e30a6d345b3347cd2ee355a7f195c719174e4e32dc4819a4d36ca4d9b0f9\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe `development-harness` plugin references 24 distinct `ADR-*` identifiers across its Python and markdown files. Only two have authoritative definition documents:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adr-9-close-resolve-semantics.md`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-1770-1-single-writer-task-backend.md`\\n\\nEvery other referenced ADR exists only as a code comment citing it. This includes the entire design rationale for the progressive-disclosure subsystem (`ADR-2`, `ADR-3`, `ADR-4`, `ADR-7`), which `disclosure_handler.py`, `disclosure_types.py`, and `ordinal_mapper.py` cite as governing behavior \u2014 notably ADR-7, which dictates that a node with sub-heading children returns empty content plus a child map rather than prose.\\n\\nThree colliding numbering schemes are in use simultaneously, making a bare reference ambiguous:\\n\\n- Bare sequential: `ADR-1` \u2026 `ADR-9`\\n- Zero-padded: `ADR-001` \u2026 `ADR-010`\\n- Issue-scoped: `ADR-2509-1` \u2026 `ADR-2509-5`, `ADR-1770-1`, plus `ADR-DN-1`\\n\\nA bare `ADR-3` in a code comment cannot be resolved to a specific decision by any reader.\\n\\n### Concrete consequence observed\\n\\nWhile investigating the backlog-integrity issue cluster, a design question arose: the read path addresses nodes by depth-aware dot-path ordinal (`navigate=\\\"4.0.1\\\"`), while the write path addresses only flat section names (`section=\\\"Concerns\\\"`), so a node that can be read has no corresponding write address. Determining whether this asymmetry is a deliberate architectural boundary or simply unimplemented required consulting the disclosure ADRs \u2014 which do not exist as documents. The question is currently unanswerable from the repository record.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Every `ADR-*` identifier referenced in plugin code resolves to an authoritative document, OR the reference is removed/corrected if it cites a decision that was never actually made\\n- [ ] A single numbering scheme is chosen and the colliding schemes reconciled (or each scheme's namespace is explicitly documented so a bare reference is unambiguous)\\n- [ ] The disclosure subsystem's ADRs (`ADR-2`, `ADR-3`, `ADR-4`, `ADR-7` as cited in `disclosure_handler.py` / `ordinal_mapper.py` / `disclosure_types.py`) are written down, since they govern currently-shipping behavior\\n- [ ] The read/write addressing asymmetry is explicitly recorded as either a deliberate boundary (with rationale) or a known gap\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs an **agent or maintainer reasoning about the backlog subsystem's design**, I want **the ADRs referenced throughout the code to actually exist as documents** so that **a design question can be answered from the record instead of being undecidable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Backlog-integrity investigation session, 2026-08-20 \u2014 follow-on from the #2956/#2970/#2974/#2969 cluster\\n- **Priority**: P2 \u2014 no runtime defect; blocks design decisions rather than execution\\n- **Reproduction**: `grep -rhoE \\\"ADR-[0-9A-Za-z-]+\\\" plugins/development-harness/ | sort -u` yields the referenced set; compare against files under `plugins/development-harness/docs/`\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"9b1ce53dd9e75ddb2d75ee0e6a5a360e58e1dc16f24ff7cc12e1bf8c8d954962\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPuh9nA\"}"}